= 1
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=1}

Use the <Holstein–Primakoff transformation> only on the physical occupation sector $0\le n\le2S$, with $n=a^\dagger a$ and $S^-=a^\dagger\sqrt{2S-n}$. Acting on number states gives$$
S^+|n\rangle=\sqrt{n(2S-n+1)}|n-1\rangle,\quad S^-|n\rangle=\sqrt{(n+1)(2S-n)}|n+1\rangle.
$$
Consequently $S^+S^-=(n+1)(2S-n)$ and $S^-S^+=n(2S-n+1)$, so$$
\boxed{[S^+,S^-]=2(S-n)=2S^z,\qquad[S^z,S^\pm]=\pm S^\pm.}
$$
The endpoint factors keep the spin operators inside the physical sector. The square-root ordering matters; replacing it by an unrestricted linear oscillator would not give the exact finite-spin algebra.

= a
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For the even periodic chain, every classical <Néel state> $\mathbf S_m=(-1)^mS\mathbf n$ has energy $-JNS^2$, with arbitrary unit vector $\mathbf n$. Thus there is a **continuous sphere of classical orientations**, not merely two Ising-like states. Opposite staggered directions are connected by a global spin rotation.

A quantum Néel product is not an eigenstate: transverse exchange flips opposite neighbouring spins. The ground state therefore contains quantum superpositions and zero-point <spin waves>, reducing the classical staggered moment. A finite isotropic balanced chain has a symmetry-invariant ground state; a symmetry-broken reference is an approximation for analysing fluctuations, not its exact finite-system eigenstate.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Rotate alternate sites by $\pi$ about a transverse axis so all local reference spins point up. Choosing the transverse axes to give positive anomalous coupling, the <linear spin-wave approximation> yields$$
H=-JNS^2+JS\sum_m[2a_m^\dagger a_m+a_ma_{m+1}+a_m^\dagger a_{m+1}^\dagger]+O(S^0).
$$
Use lattice spacing one and $a_m=N^{-1/2}\sum_ke^{ikm}a_k$, with **all $N$ momenta** $k=2\pi j/N$, $j=-N/2,\ldots,N/2-1$, in $[-\pi,\pi)$. Then $\gamma_k=\cos k$ and$$
H=-JNS^2+JS\sum_k[2a_k^\dagger a_k+\gamma_k(a_ka_{-k}+a_k^\dagger a_{-k}^\dagger)].
$$
Reordering $a_{-k}a_{-k}^\dagger=a_{-k}^\dagger a_{-k}+1$ gives the requested Nambu form with constant $-JNS(S+1)$. Its extra $JSN$ restores $-JNS^2$ before diagonalization. This full-zone convention has no additional sublattice or half-zone factor.

= c
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

For real even $\theta_k$, the <Bogoliubov transformation> gives$$
[\alpha_k,\alpha_q^\dagger]=(\cosh^2\theta_k-\sinh^2\theta_k)\delta_{kq}=\delta_{kq},\qquad[\alpha_k,\alpha_q]=0.
$$
Equivalently, its Nambu matrix obeys **$T_k\sigma_zT_k^\dagger=\sigma_z$**. The bosonic canonical metric is preserved; $T$ need not be unitary in the ordinary positive-definite metric.

= d
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

The inverse transformation has negative off-diagonal entries. Choosing $\tanh2\theta_k=\gamma_k$ removes anomalous terms and gives$$
\boxed{\omega_k=2JS\sqrt{1-\gamma_k^2}=2JS|\sin k|,\qquad H=-JNS(S+1)+\sum_k\omega_k(\alpha_k^\dagger\alpha_k+1/2)+O(S^0).}
$$
There are gapless points at $k=0$ and the zone boundary; near zero, $\omega_k\simeq2JS|k|$, a linear <antiferromagnetic spin wave> with velocity $2JS$ in the stated units. The zero modes are collective rotations: their divergent squeezing cannot be treated as ordinary normalized independent oscillator vacua. A finite-size cutoff or infinitesimal symmetry-breaking field regulates them.

As a constant-term check, the continuum spin-wave vacuum energy per site is $-JS(S+1)+(2/\pi)JS$. This is a spin-wave prediction, not an exact spectrum for every finite-$S$ one-dimensional chain.

