{source=paper-1-qcm2014-instanton.py}

= b
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

A <coherent state> is an eigenstate of an annihilation operator. Bosonic unnormalized states have exponential overlap and the Gaussian resolution of identity used above; their overcompleteness supplies a phase-space integration measure, not an orthonormal state count. For a normal-ordered many-body Hamiltonian, coherent-state insertion gives the <bosonic coherent-state path integral>$$
Z=\int_{\rm periodic}\mathcal D(\bar\phi,\phi)\exp\!\left[-\int_0^\beta d\tau\left(\sum_i\bar\phi_i\partial_\tau\phi_i+H_N(\bar\phi,\phi)-\mu\sum_i\bar\phi_i\phi_i\right)\right].
$$
Constants in $H_N$ must be retained. The exact time-sliced prescription places creation and annihilation symbols on adjacent slices; discarding that prescription can change ordering corrections, as the oscillator example demonstrates. The derivative term is first order because the fields describe phase space.

Fermionic coherent states instead use <Grassmann variables>. Their Gaussian identity is $\int d\bar\psi\,d\psi\,e^{-\bar\psi A\psi}=\det A$, whereas complex bosons give $(\det A)^{-1}$. Anticommutation in the trace produces **antiperiodic** fields. Accordingly bosons have $\nu_n=2\pi n/\beta$, fermions $\nu_n=(2n+1)\pi/\beta$, in these inverse-energy time units.

For one mode of grand-canonical energy $\xi$, the products give $Z_B=(1-e^{-\beta\xi})^{-1}$ and $Z_F=1+e^{-\beta\xi}$; convergence requires $\xi>0$ for the bosonic Gaussian. Correlation functions follow by source differentiation. Quartic interactions can be treated perturbatively or decoupled with a <Hubbard–Stratonovich transformation>; a saddle then describes an order parameter and quadratic fluctuations its collective modes. Such saddles require stability and an appropriate control parameter; the functional notation alone is not an exact solution of an interacting problem.

= c
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

For a dilute three-dimensional <weakly interacting Bose gas> with repulsive short-range scattering, use a contact coupling $g=4\pi\hbar^2a_s/m>0$ and $na_s^3\ll1$. In imaginary time $\tau_E$, the grand-canonical action is$$
S_E=\int_0^{\beta\hbar}d\tau_E\int d^3x\left[\bar\psi\hbar\partial_{\tau_E}\psi+\frac{\hbar^2}{2m}|\nabla\psi|^2-\mu|\psi|^2+\frac g2|\psi|^4\right],\qquad Z=\int\mathcal D\psi\,e^{-S_E/\hbar}.
$$
The uniform saddle has $n_0=\mu/g$. Its real-time equation is the <Gross–Pitaevskii equation>; quantum depletion means condensate and total density need not coincide exactly.

Writing $\psi=\sqrt{n_0}+\delta\psi$ and retaining quadratic fluctuations gives a Nambu kernel with diagonal $\epsilon_k+gn_0$, anomalous coupling $gn_0$ and $\epsilon_k=\hbar^2k^2/(2m)$. The <Bogoliubov quasiparticle dispersion> is$$
\boxed{E_k=\sqrt{\epsilon_k(\epsilon_k+2gn_0)},\qquad E_k\simeq\hbar c_s|k|,\quad c_s=\sqrt{gn_0/m}.}
$$
Phase and density fluctuations form the gapless sound mode associated with broken global $U(1)$ symmetry. At large momentum the spectrum crosses towards particle behaviour; the <healing length> is $\xi=\hbar/\sqrt{2mgn_0}$. The depletion integral $\int d^3k\,v_k^2/(2\pi)^3$ is finite in three dimensions and gives $(n-n_0)/n\simeq8\sqrt{na_s^3}/(3\sqrt\pi)$ to leading order. The contact coupling denotes a scattering-length-renormalized interaction; naive ultraviolet zero-point integrals require renormalization.

The condensate phase gives <superfluid velocity> $\mathbf v_s=(\hbar/m)\nabla\theta$ and quantized circulation $\oint\mathbf v_s\cdot d\mathbf l=2\pi\hbar\ell/m$. The <Landau criterion>, $v_c=\min_kE_k/(\hbar k)$, gives $c_s$ for this homogeneous Bogoliubov spectrum. Boundaries and vortex nucleation can cause dissipation below that value, so it is not a universal measured critical speed. Condensation and superfluid stiffness are related but distinct; dimensional fluctuations can remove true condensate order while retaining superfluid responses.

